# apps/cms (Jest 26.x / CRA-era @types) and apps/public (Jest 30.x / current
# @types) both depend, transitively, on several packages under the same name
# but incompatible major versions (jest and its whole @jest/* family,
# @types/react, @types/react-dom, @types/jest, expect, pretty-format,
# babel-*-jest). By default pnpm hoists every package (direct or transitive)
# into a single shared "compatibility" folder
# (node_modules/.pnpm/node_modules/<pkg>) so legacy tools that do a bare
# `require('pkg')` walk-up (like CRA/react-scripts, and apparently Next.js's
# own next/jest + TypeScript's automatic type-acquisition) can still find
# them without declaring them as real dependencies. When two incompatible
# versions of the same package exist in the workspace, pnpm's choice of which
# one lands in that shared folder is arbitrary/non-deterministic — and it
# picked the *wrong* (apps/cms, jest 26.x-era) version for several packages,
# which broke apps/public's jest/tsc runs with confusing "cannot read
# testEnvironmentOptions" and "ReactNode is not assignable to ReactNode"
# errors that had nothing to do with our own config.
#
# Excluding these families from the shared hoist forces every consumer to
# resolve them only through its own real (declared) dependency graph, which
# pnpm always gets right. apps/cms already declares jest/jest-environment-jsdom
# directly (see apps/cms/package.json) for the one thing CRA's own preflight
# check needs from this shared folder, so this exclusion does not change
# apps/cms's behavior — it only removes the ambiguous, wrongly-resolved
# entries that were breaking apps/public.
hoist-pattern[]=*
hoist-pattern[]=!jest
hoist-pattern[]=!jest-*
hoist-pattern[]=!@jest/*
hoist-pattern[]=!babel-jest
hoist-pattern[]=!babel-preset-jest
hoist-pattern[]=!babel-plugin-jest-hoist
hoist-pattern[]=!expect
hoist-pattern[]=!pretty-format
hoist-pattern[]=!@types/react
hoist-pattern[]=!@types/react-dom
hoist-pattern[]=!@types/jest
